Subject: [PATCH] utils: a timer never joins its own thread, and the enrollee's chirp wait no longer owns itself

ThreadedTimer::stop() joined its thread from any caller. A periodic callback that returns false
calls it on the timer's own thread, and the enrollee's 10 s wait after a chirp response held the
last reference to its own timer, so that timer was destroyed, and stopped, on its own thread when
the callback ended: std::thread throws "Resource deadlock avoided" and the process aborts, about
10 s after every chirp response (DPP over Ethernet). The thread handle also had no lock, while the
chirp wait's thread restarts the search timer that the 1905 thread stops.

- The timer: a stop or a restart takes the old thread's handle under a lock and joins it outside,
  or detaches it when the caller is that thread. Each thread has its own state (alive, a condition
  variable), so a thread still finishing never takes a later start's for its own, and a stop
  wakes a sleeping thread at once instead of waiting out its delay.
- The enrollee: the 10 s wait is its own member timer, stopped with the search timer first in its
  destructor, and it copies the sender's MAC instead of keeping a pointer into the frame.

Tests (test_l1_timer.cpp): a periodic callback that stops its own timer; a one-shot owned by its
callback; stop() waking a 10 s sleep; starts and stops from two threads. The original timer aborts
on the first two; the new one passes them, and ThreadSanitizer (GCC 13, clang 18) reports nothing.
---
